1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,599
Episode Seven gave behavior a name.
Methods let us calculate a total once and
call it from checkout, invoice, and
refund. That solves duplicated verbs.

2
00:00:10,719 --> 00:00:23,640
It does not solve another kind of
duplication that shows up the moment you
have more than a few values of the same
kind. Suppose you need three quiz scores.
You could write score1, score2, score3.

3
00:00:23,759 --> 00:00:34,799
Then a fourth quiz appears. Then you need
to average them, sort them, or pass them
into a method that should not grow a new
parameter every semester. Separate
variables do not scale.

4
00:00:34,919 --> 00:00:50,559
You need many values under one name,
reachable by position. That need is an
array. An array is a fixed-length sequence
of elements of one type. You create it
with a length, and that length does not
grow later.

5
00:00:50,679 --> 00:01:05,279
Indexes start at zero. The last valid
index is length - 1. Those three facts —
fixed length, zero-based indexes, typed
slots — explain most beginner array bugs
before they happen. Look at this java for
a moment.

6
00:01:05,399 --> 00:01:17,799
scores is one variable. Inside it live
three ints. scores.length is a field, not
a method — that detail matters when you
later meet List.size(). Reading scores[0]
is the first element.

7
00:01:17,919 --> 00:01:27,959
Asking for scores[3] throws, because three
is past the end. Off-by-one bugs are what
happen when zero-based indexing meets a
human habit of counting from one.

8
00:01:28,079 --> 00:01:39,039
Try averaging without an array and you
feel the pain immediately: score1 + score2
+ score3 does not generalize. With an
array you loop. Look at this java for a
moment.

9
00:01:39,159 --> 00:01:50,159
Notice the cast to double — Episode Four's
integer-division trap still applies. The
array did not invent new arithmetic; it
gave the loop a single place to read from.

10
00:01:50,279 --> 00:02:04,599
Once the happy path is clear, helpers save
you from hand-rolling common work. Look at
this java for a moment. Arrays.sort
rearranges the same array in place.
Arrays.toString gives a readable print.

11
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:17,279
If you call System.out.println(nums)
directly, you usually see a type-and-hash
style string, not the contents — another
beginner surprise that looks like a broken
array when the array is fine.

12
00:02:17,399 --> 00:02:29,639
What if the data is not a single line of
scores but a grid — seats in a theater,
pixels, a small table of daily
temperatures by week? Java models that as
arrays of arrays. Look at this java for a
moment.

13
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:42,519
week[1] is the second row. week[1][0] is
the first value in that row. Rows can even
have different lengths — "ragged" arrays —
which is powerful and easy to misuse.

14
00:02:42,639 --> 00:02:47,639
When you loop a grid, nest carefully:
outer loop over rows, inner over
week[r].length.

15
00:02:47,759 --> 00:02:57,959
Now the design question arrays force: what
happens when the number of scores is not
known up front, or keeps changing as users
add and remove items? Arrays do not grow.

16
00:02:58,079 --> 00:03:09,399
To "add" an element you allocate a larger
array and copy. That is fine for fixed
tables or buffers with a known capacity.
When size changes as a normal part of the
feature, prefer a List.

17
00:03:09,519 --> 00:03:19,279
Arrays remain the fixed-length backbone
under many collections, but they are not
the default tool for an open-ended
shopping cart. Assuming arrays grow is one
of the most common early mistakes.

18
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:32,479
Someone writes nums[nums.length] = 99
hoping to extend the array and gets an
exception instead. The honest move is
either a correctly sized array from the
start or a List that is allowed to grow.

19
00:03:32,599 --> 00:03:45,599
Interviewers often ask array.length versus
List.size(): arrays expose a length field
because size is part of the array object;
collections expose size() because their
size is behavior that can change.

20
00:03:45,719 --> 00:03:58,879
Methods packaged repeated behavior. Arrays
package repeated values by position. When
the size must change as a product
requirement, lists take over. And yet many
programs are not mainly lists of numbers.

21
00:03:58,999 --> 00:04:10,119
They are full of text — names, messages,
ids, error lines. You can store characters
in a char[], but almost nobody wants to
manage text that way day to day.

22
00:04:10,239 --> 00:04:17,279
Java's String type looks friendly until
modification and comparison surprise you.
That surprise is where strings begin.
